WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.636
Broad object updates can undo careful
boundary design.

2
00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:06.588
Assigning a request body onto an existing
user

3
00:00:06.588 --> 00:00:10.093
copies whatever fields the input supplies
into that object.

4
00:00:10.093 --> 00:00:14.336
Spreading the body into an update object
also propagates its fields.

5
00:00:14.336 --> 00:00:16.734
Recursive merges, class transformation,
automatic entity

6
00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:19.409
hydration, and object relational mapping
helpers can

7
00:00:19.409 --> 00:00:21.808
extend the effect into nested structures.

8
00:00:21.808 --> 00:00:24.851
Review the destination and the copying
behavior, not

9
00:00:24.851 --> 00:00:27.988
just the presence of a validation call
nearby.

10
00:00:27.988 --> 00:00:31.862
Define a public input contract separately
from the persistence entity.

11
00:00:31.862 --> 00:00:34.444
Making every entity property optional does
not

12
00:00:34.444 --> 00:00:37.212
decide which fields a caller should
control.

13
00:00:37.212 --> 00:00:40.440
Internal roles, ownership, audit fields,
and state flags

14
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.576
should not become public update options by
accident.

15
00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:46.159
Build the update explicitly from validated
fields

16
00:00:46.159 --> 00:00:48.926
and obtain protected values from trusted
context.

17
00:00:48.926 --> 00:00:52.431
Preserve the intended distinction between
omitted and null values.

18
00:00:52.431 --> 00:00:55.937
Explicit mapping makes the allowed
mutation visible during review.

19
00:00:55.937 --> 00:00:59.442
Coercion is a design choice that needs
precise rules.

20
00:00:59.442 --> 00:01:03.223
Converting a documented numeric string can
be appropriate, but accepting

21
00:01:03.223 --> 00:01:06.729
ambiguous input can change meaning before
later checks run.

22
00:01:06.729 --> 00:01:10.510
JavaScript treats the nonempty string
false as truthy, so a

23
00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:14.385
general boolean conversion does not
implement a textual boolean contract.

24
00:01:14.385 --> 00:01:16.414
Specify accepted spellings, numeric
ranges,

25
00:01:16.414 --> 00:01:18.535
precision requirements, and date formats.

26
00:01:18.535 --> 00:01:22.410
Keep identifiers as strings when numeric
conversion would lose information.

27
00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.546
Validate the normalized result and test
rejected forms.

28
00:01:25.546 --> 00:01:27.759
Object keys also deserve attention when

29
00:01:27.759 --> 00:01:29.789
data passes through generic utilities.

30
00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:32.371
JSON parsing alone does not automatically
pollute

31
00:01:32.371 --> 00:01:35.046
prototypes; risks can arise when later
copying

32
00:01:35.046 --> 00:01:37.814
or merging interprets prototype sensitive
keys unsafely.

33
00:01:37.814 --> 00:01:40.581
Reject unsupported keys, constrain
legitimate dictionaries, and

34
00:01:40.581 --> 00:01:43.348
use suitable data only structures or maps.

35
00:01:43.348 --> 00:01:46.853
These controls complement explicit mapping
rather than replacing it.

36
00:01:46.853 --> 00:01:49.067
A validated transfer object should remain

37
00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:51.465
a small description of permitted input.

38
00:01:51.465 --> 00:01:55.155
It should not acquire the ability to
mutate an entire

39
00:01:55.155 --> 00:01:58.660
model merely because a helper makes that
operation convenient.

